# Source ranges for the MkDocs toolchain. The file that is INSTALLED is the
# compiled docs/requirements.txt beside it, which pins every direct and
# transitive distribution by version AND by hash (#1509); this one only says
# what the site is allowed to be built with.
#
# Regenerate after every edit here:
#
#     task docs:lock
#
# which is `uv pip compile docs/requirements.in --universal
# --python-version 3.11 --generate-hashes -o docs/requirements.txt`. The two
# flags are measured, not stylistic: the release lane installs this set TWICE,
# once on the runner (Python 3.14, `reusable-mkdocs.yaml` pre-build) and once
# inside `mhausenblas/mkdocs-deploy-gh-pages`, whose image is
# `squidfunk/mkdocs-material:9.0.6` — Alpine, CPython 3.11.0, measured with
# `docker run --entrypoint sh ... -c 'python -V'`. `--universal` emits one file
# valid for both, including the musllinux wheel hashes that musl container
# needs (verified against PyPI's file list for charset-normalizer 3.5.1: all 60
# musllinux hashes are present), and `--python-version 3.11` keeps the resolve
# inside the older of the two interpreters.
# EVERY ENTRY CARRIES A FLOOR AND A CEILING (#1601). NFR-009 §2.3.1 decides that
# §2.1 binds the COMPILE SOURCE of a class (a) list as well as the compiled
# output: the hashes below in docs/requirements.txt bind the bytes installed
# TODAY, and only the ranges here bind what the NEXT `task docs:lock` may
# choose. Without a ceiling that next run can pull a MAJOR of eight plugins at
# once, and the diff showing it is tens of kilobytes of hash lines nobody reads
# line by line. `src/backend/tests/unit/guards/test_docs_requirements_source_bounds.py`
# holds it.
#
# The ceilings added in #1601 are the CURRENTLY RESOLVED major of each package,
# so they describe the set already installed rather than moving it: recompiling
# after adding them left docs/requirements.txt byte-identical (measured
# 2026-09-20). Raising one is a reviewed decision, which is the whole point.
# The floors are left exactly as they were — this change adds ceilings, it does
# not re-resolve anything. Two of them are therefore far below what resolves
# today (mkdocstrings >=0.24.0 resolves to 1.0.6, pymdown-extensions >=10.5 to
# 12.0.1); raising a floor changes nothing about what uv picks (it always picks
# the newest allowed) and would have made this a version bump in disguise.

mkdocs>=1.6,<2.0
mkdocs-material>=9.7,<10.0
mkdocs-static-i18n>=1.2.0,<2.0.0  # resolves to 1.3.1
mkdocstrings[python]>=0.24.0,<2.0.0  # resolves to 1.0.6
mkdocs-git-revision-date-localized-plugin>=1.2.0,<2.0.0  # resolves to 1.6.0
mkdocs-awesome-pages-plugin>=2.9.0,<3.0.0  # resolves to 2.10.1
mkdocs-minify-plugin>=0.7.0,<1.0.0  # resolves to 0.8.0
mkdocs-redirects>=1.2.0,<2.0.0  # resolves to 1.2.3
pymdown-extensions>=10.5,<13.0.0  # resolves to 12.0.1
mike>=2.0.0,<3.0.0  # resolves to 2.2.0
mkdocs-literate-nav>=0.6,<1.0
pyyaml>=6.0,<7.0
